  "slug": "docking-dynamics-049f",
  "summary": "Three preprints probe the validity of sequence-based and omics inference, from non-coding DNA compressibility to epistasis calls and random-signature predictivity.",
  "synthesis": "The current items share a methodological concern with what genomic and omics data can support inferentially, rather than a common biological target. One preprint proposes a layered computational framework for non-coding DNA and tests sequence compressibility against reporter-assay variant effects, reporting no support at the tested level. A second applies Fisher exact and DISCOVER screens to 141 E. coli ALE experiments in ALEdb to nominate epistatic gene pairs, with power analysis. A third argues that random omics marker sets often reach optimal predictivity because optimal feature sets form large equivalence classes. Together they emphasize null models and statistical power. All are preprints, and the summaries report no head-to-head benchmarks against established tools. The earlier mutation-signature and TF-binding emphasis is not supported by these items.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
